He at least tried. That's much more worth than your postings are.

> Dude's asking when these slow-ass makers of this game are going to ever
> release another version. It's been three years,

Three years? o_O

> it's a legitimate complaint.

He wasn't complaining. He was asking nicely.

> The source code is public, so if the DevTeam actually cared about the game
> of Nethack they should announce that they're too fucking lazy to make another

It's of course no secret that a lot of people are unhappy about the
situation.

There hasn't been a new release in almost six years now. There are a
bunch of bugs in vanilla that are labeled "fixed" on the home page but
we neither have a bugfix release nor patches from the DevTeam to fix
even only the crashing bugs. There is no accessible repository where
we could see the advanced dev version they have been working on
(according to their own words).

But, you know what? That's completely irrelevant because they don't
have to and don't need to do anything. It's that simple.

> one and let some other team of programmers take over the offical version.

NetHack is open source and the answer for open source is always:
"STFU and DIY!"

There is no anti-variant-squad that prevents anybody from releasing a
NetHack game. I'm pretty sure about that know (I guess they would have
caught Derek way sooner than me but you never know).

Just take the source and change it or if you can't program find
people who can and let them do it.

Or take Angband and mod it into a NetHack look-alike.


Nobody needs the blessing of the DevTeam. There is nothing and nobody
that could prevent someone from releasing a "NetHack 4.0.0" like Derek
once proposed. But nobody does - so it looks like nobody cares enough
to do it.

And who should that "other team of programmers" be anyway?

> It'd be nice to see Nethack stay relevant in a increasingly complex gaming
> world. That's just me, though. 

I wonder if it really were that relevant or if it's just some myth
that spread about a game that's so damn abusive about its players but
the players nevertheless play *and* love it for that.

